## 5. Testing strategy

| What | How |
|---|---|
| Image builds and runs non-root | CI job builds it on every PR (without pushing) and runs `docker run --rm img id -u` expecting `10001` |
| Image vulnerabilities | Trivy in the publish job (M11.3) |
| Migrations from zero | Existing Testcontainers tests start on an empty DB and apply every migration |
| Migrations from the previous release | Optional CI job: start PostgreSQL, migrate with the `main` image, then migrate with the PR's image, then run the smoke test. Catches migrations that only work from an empty database |
| App refuses pending migrations | Integration test with `FlywayValidateOnlyConfig` active against a DB missing the last migration |
| Full-stack smoke | `scripts/demo.sh` against Compose in CI (on `main`, or nightly if too slow for PRs) |
| Deployed smoke | `scripts/demo.sh --no-start` against the deployment after each deploy |
| Zero-downtime deploy | Manual, once: `while true; do curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' $URL/actuator/health/readiness; sleep 0.5; done` during a deploy |
| API contract | OpenAPI drift check (M11.7); Bruno collection in CI |
| Docs accuracy | Manual review against the code before marking M11.10 done |

---

## 6. Exit criteria checklist

- [x] Multi-stage Dockerfile, non-root, layered, `.dockerignore` excludes `.env`
- [x] `docker compose --profile full up` runs api, worker, migrate, Postgres, MinIO, Keycloak; `observability` adds Prometheus and Grafana
- [x] Merges to `main` push `ghcr.io/kasalmanfarcy/reconflow:sha-<commit>` after tests pass; image scanned (the workflow; it first runs once the repository is on GitHub)
- [ ] Deployed on a public HTTPS URL with managed PostgreSQL 16 and S3-compatible storage — **prepared and rehearsed locally; the deploy is an owner step** (docs/runbooks/deploy.md)
- [x] Migrations run as a separate step with the migrator role; the app only validates; every new migration has a `Rollback:` note
- [x] README rewritten; old open questions replaced by ADR links
- [x] OpenAPI spec committed and drift-checked; Swagger UI off in prod
- [x] Bruno collection walks the demo and passes
- [x] `./scripts/demo.sh` passes on a fresh stack, twice in a row, and against the deployment rehearsal (against a real deployment: owner step)
- [x] `docs/architecture.md` matches the code
- [x] `docs/trade-offs.md` written, every claim linked
- [ ] GIF or video in the README — **owner step** (docs/media/README.md); a text excerpt of a real run stands in

TASKS.md's exit criterion — *someone can clone the repo, run a single command, and watch the full workflow from upload to resolution* — is the `./scripts/demo.sh` line. Test it on a machine that has never seen the project.

---

## 7. Interview questions this phase prepares you for

**Why a multi-stage Dockerfile?**
The build needs a JDK, Maven and the source; the runtime needs only a JRE and the compiled classes. Building in one stage and copying the output into a JRE-only stage makes the image smaller and removes tools an attacker could use. It also keeps the Maven cache and source code out of the shipped image.

**What does extracting the jar into layers buy you?**
Docker reuses unchanged layers. Dependencies change rarely and the application code changes every commit, so splitting them means a typical rebuild and push only ships the small application layer instead of an 80 MB fat jar. Deploys get faster and registry storage smaller.

**How should a JVM be configured in a container?**
Let it size from the container limit with `MaxRAMPercentage` around 75, leaving room for non-heap memory, rather than hard-coding `-Xmx` into the image. Exit on OOM so the orchestrator restarts a clean process. Run as a non-root numeric UID.

**How do you run database migrations in a deployment with several instances?**
As one separate step before new instances start, using credentials that have DDL rights; the running app only has data rights and only validates that the schema is current. If the migration fails, the deploy stops and the old version keeps serving. Running migrations on every app startup means racing instances, DDL rights in every process, and crash loops on failure.

**How do you make schema changes with zero downtime?**
Expand/contract: add the new structure in a backwards-compatible way, deploy code that uses it, then remove the old structure in a later release. At every point, the current and previous code versions both work with the schema, so a rolling deploy and a one-step rollback are both safe. Also avoid long locks: concurrent index creation, `NOT VALID` constraints validated later, batched backfills, and a lock timeout.

**How do you roll back a Flyway migration?**
Flyway Community has no undo, and pre-written down-scripts are rarely tested anyway. I roll back by redeploying the previous image, which works because expand/contract keeps the previous version compatible with the new schema. Fixes to the schema itself go forward as new migrations; real data loss is handled by point-in-time recovery.

**Liveness vs readiness?**
Liveness answers "is the process stuck?" and failing it restarts the container, so it mustn't depend on the database or a DB outage restarts everything. Readiness answers "can I take traffic?" and failing it only removes the instance from the load balancer, so it should include the database.

**Why tag images with the git SHA?**
It's immutable and maps one-to-one to the code, so you always know what's running, can reproduce it, and can roll back to an exact previous build. `latest` changes under you and makes "what's deployed?" unanswerable.

**How do secrets get into your containers?**
As environment variables from the platform's secret store, never baked into the image or committed. The running app has only the DB app role's password; the migrator password is given only to the migration step. The app fails fast at startup if a required secret is missing.

**Dockerfile or buildpacks?**
Buildpacks give a good, secure image with no Dockerfile, built-in memory tuning and SBOMs, and can rebase the OS layer without rebuilding: great for many services. I wrote a Dockerfile here because I wanted to understand and be able to explain every layer.

---

## 8. Common mistakes

- **Secrets in the image**: a `COPY . .` that picks up `.env`. Deleting it in a later layer doesn't remove it.
- **Running migrations from every app instance with owner credentials**, quietly undoing M10.7.
- **Dropping or renaming a column in the same release as the code change.** The old instances still running during the deploy start failing.
- **A readiness probe that's really a liveness probe** (or the reverse), causing restart loops during a DB blip.
- **Only a `latest` tag.**
- **A demo that only works on your machine**: absolute paths, an existing `.env`, a local Maven cache, dates outside the partition window, data left over from a previous run.
- **Docs and diagrams that describe the plan instead of the code.** Interviewers who open the repo will notice the difference.
- **A README that starts with the tech stack** instead of the problem.
- **Leaving the deployment on a free tier that sleeps** while sending the link to recruiters, so the first click takes 30 seconds or times out.

---

## Resume tie-in

Each deliverable is the evidence behind a resume bullet (see the "after it's fully built" version discussed earlier). If the evidence isn't there, the bullet shouldn't be either.

| Resume claim | Backed by |
|---|---|
| Multi-tenant payment reconciliation platform (the overview bullet) | Live URL (M11.4), README pitch (M11.6), GIF (M11.12) |
| Streaming import at **[N] rows in [X s]** | `docs/benchmarks.md` (M9.6), linked from the README highlights |
| Idempotent engine, nothing lost or double-counted | `scripts/demo.sh` run twice with identical results (M11.9); property test linked from `architecture.md` |
| Exception workflow with optimistic locking and transactional audit | Demo's stale-ETag step and audit-trail output (M11.9); Bruno conflict request (M11.8); sequence diagram (M11.10) |
| Tenant isolation with RLS and cross-tenant tests | `docs/security.md` (M10), least-privilege roles in deployment (M11.4–M11.5) |
| In-house auth, then OIDC migration touching only the security package | The M10.1 ADR, linked from `docs/trade-offs.md` (M11.11) |
| Postgres job queue with leases and failure-injection tests | `architecture.md` crash/re-claim sequence diagram (M11.10), trade-offs section on Kafka (M11.11) |
| Shipped with partitioned schema, CI with Testcontainers, metrics, non-root Docker image | Dockerfile (M11.1), CI + GHCR job (M11.3), migrate step (M11.5), Grafana in Compose (M11.2) |

---

## 9. As built: where the code differs from this plan, and why

docs/ win over this plan wherever they disagree. Built in the plan's order (M11.1, M11.2, M11.5, M11.3, M11.4, M11.7, M11.9, M11.8, M11.6, M11.10, M11.11, M11.12), one commit per task. Nothing left the machine: no deploy, no image push, no repository change on GitHub; images were built and scanned locally and every stack was started under its own compose project name and removed with `down -v`.

| Plan said | Built | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **M11.1** `eclipse-temurin:25-jre` (Ubuntu) + `curl` for the health check; `-Djava.security.egd` | `25.0.5_8-jre-alpine`, both stages pinned by tag **and digest**; busybox `wget` for the health check (follows `MANAGEMENT_PORT`); no `egd` (obsolete since Java 9); `-Dmaven.test.skip` (tests compiled only in CI); `.dockerignore` an allow-list; OCI labels; Dependabot `docker` ecosystem | Smaller OS, fewer packages to patch, nothing installed. Measured: **149 MB compressed, 300 MB unpacked**, `id` = uid 10001; a rebuild after a source change reused the dependency layer. Trivy (0.76.2, local, 2026-11-16): **0 HIGH/CRITICAL, 1 MEDIUM** (zlib in the Alpine base, CVE-2026-4738, fixed in 1.4-r0: arrives with the next base digest). Building needs BuildKit (Buildx on Colima: README) |
| **M11.2** `env_file: .env` for the app; `extra_hosts: localhost:host-gateway` | Each role gets an explicit environment (never the superuser's password; migrate the migrator's only); the issuer string stays `localhost:8180` while keys come from `keycloak:8180` over the compose network (`...JWT_JWKSETURI`); `mem_limit`s; the seeder's demo guard is a property (`reconflow.seed.only-into-demo-database`) | Least privilege per role; `localhost` mapped to the host gateway is unreliable (Docker already maps it to 127.0.0.1) |
| **M11.5** `FlywayValidateOnlyConfig` with `@Profile("!migrate & !local & !test")` | `shared.db.FlywayStrategyConfig` switched by `reconflow.flyway.strategy` (`validate` in prod, `migrate` elsewhere); prod's Flyway connects **as the app role**, so `DB_MIGRATOR_*` left prod's required properties; `MigrateAndExit` exits explicitly; `FlywayValidateOnlyIT` on a separate database; `scripts/check_migration_notes.sh` in CI from V13 | Profile names live only in YAML (plans/README); validating needs only `SELECT` on the history, so the running app never sees the migrator password. Verified in compose: an api started before migrate exits 1 with Flyway's "not applied" message |
| **M11.3** `aquasecurity/trivy-action`, push job only | An `image` job on every PR (build without push, uid check, Trivy from its pinned image, `--timeout 15m`) and `publish-image` on `main` after `build` and `image`, `provenance: mode=max`, SBOM | Same pattern as gitleaks (the CLI image, no third-party action); the first local scan needed more than Trivy's default 5 min to fetch its Java DB |
| **M11.4** Render (PaaS), Keycloak as a Render service | **One VM with Docker Compose** (`deploy/compose.prod.yaml`): Traefik v3.5.6 (Let's Encrypt, HTTP→HTTPS, 51 MiB body cap, per-IP limits with a tighter one on Keycloak's token/login endpoints, `/admin` not routed), Keycloak `start` on its own database (`infra/db/bootstrap-keycloak.sql`), api/worker read-only with no capabilities, migrate as `deploy.sh`'s second stage; production realm without the password grant plus a `reconflow-cli` PKCE client (`scripts/lib/oidc.sh`); `server.forward-headers-strategy: native`; a gated `deploy.yml` workflow; `compose.local.yaml` rehearses it with PostgreSQL, MinIO and a throwaway CA | The exact files can be proven on a laptop with nothing leaving it, and the edge controls M10 left open need a proxy of our own. Rehearsed: all stages pass; HSTS present through the proxy, `/admin` 404, password grant `invalid_client`, PKCE login, `POST /members` through the internal Admin API (201), 54 MB upload 413 at Traefik, login burst 429, `demo.sh` twice. Not done (owner): the deploy itself, so no public URL and no measured deploy gap |
| **M11.7** `@Operation` on the non-obvious cases | `@ApiResponses` on every endpoint (success + main errors from api-design §8.1), a customizer adding 401/403/429 and the ProblemDetail schema; its own filter chain with a CSP that lets Swagger UI load; `OpenApiSpecIT` also checks completeness against the handler mappings and status codes against api-design §8.1 | A spec that's only a drift check would still be free to say `200` for a `202` |
| **M11.9** Dataset of ~20 rows, 12 matches; `--no-start` against a deployment | 19 ledger and 22 gateway rows (12 exact matches + 1 normalized), `expected.json` with imports, summary and open cases; login by PKCE; `DemoDatasetIT` checks the numbers and the second run on every build | The script has to work against a deployed realm; a demo whose numbers aren't in the build drifts |
| **M11.8** Bruno, 10 requests | 15 (three role logins, a stale-ETag request), local realm only (password grant) | Bruno CLI 4.2.1 headless: 15/15 against the full profile |
| **M11.12** GIF | Recording instructions; the README shows an excerpt of a real run | Recording needs a person at a terminal |
| — | CONTRIBUTING: `perf` type; migration rules (expand/contract, locks, rollback note) | M9's commits used `perf`; M11.5's rules needed a home |

Fixed along the way: `deploy.sh` reported success before Traefik's health check (every 5 s) had re-admitted the new api, so a smoke test right after it got `503`; it now waits until an anonymous call through the edge answers `401`. `.env.example` lacked `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, which compose requires whichever profiles run, so a fresh clone couldn't `docker compose up` at all.

**Final checks (2026-11-16):** `./mvnw clean verify` passed, 380 unit tests and 749 integration tests, 0 failures or skips. The rehearsal from an empty state: deploy stages all exit 0; readiness and liveness `UP`; through the edge `401` with HSTS, HTTP `301`, password grant `invalid_client`, `/admin` `404`, `/actuator/prometheus` `401`, `/v3/api-docs` and `/swagger-ui.html` `404` even authenticated, `POST /members` `201`, a 54 MB upload `413`; `demo.sh` twice, all checks passed, the second reusing both batches. Then `down -v`.

Tests added: `FlywayValidateOnlyIT`, `ProductionRealmTest`, `OpenApiSpecIT`, `DemoDatasetIT`, a demo-guard case in `LocalSeederIT`; `CrossTenantCoverageIT` exempts the OpenAPI paths.

Not done, needing the owner: the deployment and its public URL, the GitHub settings (required checks including `image`, secret scanning, Dependabot, `NVD_API_KEY`, GHCR visibility, the `production` environment and deploy secrets), the demo GIF. A zero-downtime api deploy would need a second api replica (runbook §6).

